{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 6, :comments [{:quotes [{:he "לכה נא", :translit "lekha na", :gloss "come now"}], :mechanisms [{:kind :other, :note "Letter heh at end of לכה signals four standards plus mixed multitude"}], :frame {:text "Israel traveled in the wilderness in four standards (Numbers 2), each associated with a tribe; the mixed multitude (ערב רב) that left Egypt with Israel is described in rabbinic sources as attaching itself to Israel like a tail.", :sources ["Numbers 2" "Exodus 12:38" "Jerusalem Talmud Ta'anit 4:5"]}} {:quotes [{:he "ארה לי", :translit "arah li", :gloss "curse for me"}], :mechanisms [{:kind :other, :note "Form ארה (instead of אור) signals that Balaam's mouth was his undoing"}], :frame {:text "Balak asked Balaam to curse Israel (Numbers 22:6), but God turned the curses into blessings (Numbers 22–24). Balaam's own mouth, intended for cursing, became the instrument of Israel's blessing.", :sources ["Numbers 22:6" "Numbers 24:10" "Deuteronomy 23:6" "Nehemiah 13:2"]}} {:quotes [{:he "מבורך", :translit "mevorakh", :gloss "blessed"} {:he "אשר תברך מברך", :translit "asher tevarekh mevorkhakh", :gloss "whom you bless is blessed"} {:he "יהי שם ה' מבורך", :translit "yehi shem ha-shem mevorkhakh", :gloss "may the name of the Lord be blessed"} {:he "יהי שם ה' מב ורך", :translit "yehi shem ha-shem me-v-orekh", :gloss "may the name of the Lord — from-curse-and-bless"}], :mechanisms [{:kind :masorah, :count 3, :occurrences ["אשר תברך מברך" "יהי שם ה' מבורך" "ואף כי נבלה"]} {:kind :other, :note "Reading מבורך as מארר ומברך — he curses and blesses using the divine name"}], :frame {:text "Balaam possessed knowledge of the precise moment when God is angry, enabling him to curse at that instant (Berakhot 7a, Sanhedrin 105b). The moment of divine anger is identified in Berakhot 7a as the first three hours of the day. The phrase יהי שם ה' מבורך occurs in Genesis 24:27 spoken by Abraham's servant; here it is read as alluding to Balaam's technique of cursing and blessing with the divine name.", :sources ["Berakhot 7a" "Sanhedrin 105b" "Genesis 24:27"]}} {:quotes [{:he "תאור", :translit "ta'or", :gloss "you curse"} {:he "ואשר תאור", :translit "ve-asher ta'or", :gloss "and whom you curse"} {:he "לא תאור את העם", :translit "lo ta'or et ha-am", :gloss "you shall not curse the people"} {:he "ונשיא בעמך לא תאור", :translit "ve-nasi be-amekha lo ta'or", :gloss "and a prince among your people you shall not curse"}], :mechanisms [{:kind :masorah, :count 3, :occurrences ["ואשר תאור" "לא תאור את העם" "ונשיא בעמך לא תאור"]} {:kind :other, :note "Halakhic derivation from לא תאור that cursing any person is forbidden"}], :frame {:text "The verse נשיא בעמך לא תאור (Exodus 22:27) is interpreted in Sanhedrin 66a: just as cursing a judge (נשיא) is prohibited, so too cursing any person is forbidden, derived by treating the singular form as paradigmatic for all people.", :sources ["Exodus 22:27" "Sanhedrin 66a"]}} {:quotes [{:he "ואשר תאר יואר", :translit "ve-asher ta'or ye'orer", :gloss "and whom you curse is cursed"} {:he "שקיללתה את מואב", :translit "she-kilelta et mo'av", :gloss "that you cursed Moab"}], :mechanisms [{:kind :gematria, :a "ואשר תאר יואר", :b "שקיללתה את מואב"}], :frame nil}], :arithmetic [{:kind :other, :note "Letter heh at end of לכה signals four standards plus mixed multitude", :by :selah, :ran? false} {:kind :other, :note "Form ארה (instead of אור) signals that Balaam's mouth was his undoing", :by :selah, :ran? false} {:kind :masorah, :count 3, :occurrences ["אשר תברך מברך" "יהי שם ה' מבורך" "ואף כי נבלה"], :by :selah, :located [{:phrase "אשר תברך מברך", :refs ["Numbers 22:6"], :n 1, :lens :exact} {:phrase "יהי שם ה' מבורך", :refs [], :n 0, :lens :plene-insensitive} {:phrase "ואף כי נבלה", :refs [], :n 0, :lens :plene-insensitive}]} {:kind :other, :note "Reading מבורך as מארר ומברך — he curses and blesses using the divine name", :by :selah, :ran? false} {:kind :masorah, :count 3, :occurrences ["ואשר תאור" "לא תאור את העם" "ונש